          <Pad Id="IsEtfS38lM4MYF9w2R4Pf8" Bounds="814,33,653,745" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="This patch builds on the previous example and demonstrates how procedural textures can be generated and manipulated entirely on the GPU before being displayed in Skia.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The patch works as follows:&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;1. Generate a Liquish Texture: The node creates a procedural noise texture. Unlike an image loaded from disk, this texture is generated mathematically and can be animated or modified at any time. This specific node distords one noise with another ( https://thebookofshaders.com/13/ → Domain Warping). The Scale parameter controls the size of the noise features, while Color A and Color B define the colour range of the texture.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;2. Generate a Blur Mask: A circular texture is created and then blurred. The Radius parameter controls the size of the circle, while Strength determines the amount of blur.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;3. Displace the circle with the noise: The blurred circle is connected to the Displace node. Displacement offsets the texture coordinates of the circle texture, creating a distortion effect. The Offset Scale parameter controls the strength of the displacement.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;4. Blend the Original and Distorted Textures: The Blend node combines the original noise texture with the displaced version. This is optional, but it introduces a node: The Blend Operation determines how the two textures are combined, while the Factor parameter controls the strength of the blend. Experimenting with different blend modes can produce a wide variety of visual effects.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;5. Convert the Texture into an Alpha Mask: The resulting texture is passed through ColorMap and Convert to extract colour information from the GPU texture. The output is then used by SetAlpha, effectively turning the texture into an alpha mask. Bright areas become opaque, while darker areas become more transparent.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;6. Convert the Texture for Skia&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;7. Draw the Result: The image is drawn onto the Skia canvas using DrawImage. SetBlendMode controls how the image interacts with any other graphics already present on the canvas. In this example, the Luminosity blend mode is used, producing a light-based compositing effect.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;8. Render the Final Image&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This example demonstrates a common workflow in vvvv gamma: generate procedural textures in Stride, use them to create masks or effects on the GPU, convert them to Skia images, and finally combine them with other 2D graphics. This approach is highly efficient and allows complex animated effects to be created with relatively few nodes.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
